  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe main item of concern this week is a frontal passage on\nWednesday and associated rain/storm chances and the cooldown\nbehind it. We will be in a fairly zonal flow aloft leading to\nwarm days/cool nights through Tuesday. During the day Wednesday\nguidance continues to show a shortwave digging southeastward \ntowards the region. This low spins up a cyclone to our northeast\n(and trending further northeast in the guidance consensus \ncompared to 24 hours ago). The shortwave phases with another \nupper low over eastern Canada to produce broad and deep \ncyclonic flow over most of the eastern US centered over the \nupper Ohio Valley. This grabs air currently sitting over \nnorthern Canada and sends it this direction in our first \nencounter with semi-arctic airmass of the cool season.\n\nSouthwesterly winds pick up ahead of the frontal passage but\nthis results in a source airmass for advection over Texas and\nlimits available moisture return. Dewpoints there are in the mid\n50s. This results in relatively limited but non-zero surface\nbased instability with high LCL heights and 10-12 degree\ndewpoint spreads as showers and thunderstorms develop ahead of\nthe front. Coverage trends are down from 12-24 hours\nago. Available entrainment factored MLCAPE is about 300 J/kg and\nthe thermodynamic profiles look like it would be reasonable to\nexpect parcels to struggle to fire. If convection can form there\nmight be some wind risk given the deep mixing and available\nlower and deep layer shear (850 mb ~30kts or so). \n\nMuch colder air moves in behind the front with the source\nairmass currently running about 5 to 10 degrees F. Thursday\nstill looks to produce northwest winds gusting 10-20 mph with\ntemperatures in the 40s most of the day. Highs Friday look\nunlikely to break 50F as a reinforcing shot of cooler air pushes\nin. Saturday tries to warm into the 50s in most guidance but\nthis might be a little optimistic given the current projected\ncloud cover and ongoing cold air advection. We get on the warmer\nside of the ridge axis by Sunday and should start to moderate\ntemperatures.",
